WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.279
. Today, we're going to be speaking with Douglas
Murray, who is the best selling author of

00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:11.429
numerous books, including The Madness of Crowds,
The Strange Death of Europe.

00:00:11.429 --> 00:00:13.230
It's great to have you on first and foremost.

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:15.840
Very good to be with you, David.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:23.039
So we're having this conversation just a few
days before a major election here in the United

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:27.519
States, where there will be either another
four years of Donald Trump or not.

00:00:27.519 --> 00:00:32.020
There will be either more Republican control
of the Senate or not.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:36.551
And really, a lot of the next two, four, six
years in the United States depends on the

00:00:36.551 --> 00:00:38.570
outcome of this election.

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:48.370
We've seen in other sort of Western Democratic
allies also sort of turning points or potential

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:53.239
inflection points in terms of a more nationalist
move or not.

00:00:53.239 --> 00:00:59.920
I guess with you, where I would want to start
is what do you see as the state of conservatism

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:01.230
right now?

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:06.540
And does that relate in any way to Donald
Trump or is Donald Trump not really part of

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:13.180
that movement as some traditional conservatives
in the United States feel?

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:18.430
It's it's a broad and complex question, and
it needs a broad and complex answer.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:23.400
I mean, the short answer is, I think there
has been slightly uncomfortable alliance in

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:28.041
recent years between Donald Trump and the
Republican Party and the broader conservative

00:01:28.041 --> 00:01:30.370
movement in the US.

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:35.370
And whether that remains or uncomfortable
becomes more uncomfortable or leads to a tiresome,

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:36.370
terrible bloodletting.

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:42.320
Well, I suppose depend on whether or not he
wins the election.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:43.830
Not many days time.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:47.000
I think that there will be if he doesn't win
the election.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.460
I think there'll be considerable going over
of exactly what it is that happened in the

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:59.930
last four years and more in American Republican
politics that allowed for this to happen.

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:05.200
If he does win, of course, that all changes
because everybody migrates towards victors.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.420
And many people on the conservative side in
American politics, many people who were unsure,

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:15.430
for instance, about joining his administration
or being aligned with it in the first four

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:18.530
years may decide to make some peace with it.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:20.820
Not everybody, but some more people will.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:24.530
I don't think, by the way, that although there
is an attempt always by the international

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:29.910
media to always draw sort of clear lines,
you know, we always like it to be, you know,

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:33.250
the world's turning left or the world's turning
right.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:34.680
And it never quite works like that.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:39.760
I think there are trends you can see, but
I don't actually think that Donald Trump is

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:44.730
part of any broader conservative or right
wing tendency.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:50.790
He is, whether you're a Meyera detractor or
one of the confused people in the middle.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.900
I mean, he is a very unusual force.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:58.540
I do not think he is in any ways replicated
in other countries.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:03.490
He's not he's not replicated in my own country,
for instance, in the United Kingdom.

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:06.640
So it's very hard to draw broader lessons
away from Donald Trump.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.670
Yeah, I completely agree with you that when
I look at Donald Trump and I look at what

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:15.880
we're sort of more traditional conservative
principles, I don't see anything there.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:20.510
Sometimes by sheer odds, he will stumble across
something that sort of seems to fit.

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:26.140
And for the most part, I see kind of buffoonery
in a dark room stumbling around and sometimes

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:27.350
hitting a light switch.

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:34.710
But that sort of gets to maybe a more forward
looking question, which is I see you as someone

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:40.650
who, although I think we would, you know,
agree about a few things, sort of, but have

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:42.480
significant political disagreements.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:50.270
I see your brand of conservatism as something
that is almost directly antithetical to most

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:53.020
of what Donald Trump's policy has been and
make.

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:55.020
My curiosity is.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:04:03.319
Do you think that it makes sense for American
conservatives to align with Trump and sort

00:04:03.319 --> 00:04:09.410
of you try to use him to the extent they can
to accomplish, for example, the confirmation

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:14.560
of judges, which which really has been a major
success for the Republican Party under Trump,

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:17.180
because he'll nominate whoever they tell him
to nominate?

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:20.120
Or is that dangerous for the future of your
own movement?

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:24.780
Well, first of all, I'm not a part of a movement.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:29.740
Conservatism is a very tricky beast to last
sue because conservatives conserve different

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:31.680
things in different countries.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:36.440
What conservatives are conserving in France
is different from what a conservative is conserving

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:41.470
in Britain or Belgium or Denmark, let alone
the United States or anywhere else.

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:45.460
It's not quite the same to that extent with
what's become known, I think erroneously as

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:50.050
liberalism or leftism, which tends to have
the same set of demands.

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:52.900
It's a virtue and device of the left.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:55.130
I think conservatism is harder to.

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:58.270
To sum up like that is an international trend.

00:04:58.270 --> 00:04:59.270
You're right.

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:01.470
By the way, that Donald Trump is unusual.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:06.350
I don't think it's quite correct to say that
he has sort of nothing in common with conservatism

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:07.840
in the United States.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:16.669
I mean, certain obvious aspects of his agenda
are very conservative.

00:05:16.669 --> 00:05:19.910
He he advocates borders.

00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:24.000
And now I mean, by the way, that's that didn't
used to be, you know, seen as being a particularly

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.000
right wing thing.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:31.170
But it is it has become a conservative aspect
to be broadly against open borders or, you

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:36.820
know, more in favor of of of stricter immigration
policies.

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:39.530
I guess I don't listen.

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:43.990
I'm just not so sure that that is actually
what makes him stand out as conservative.

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:49.190
Because when I see the Democratic proposals,
you know, on Dacca, for example, or I get

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:54.230
into details about it, I'm not seeing anybody
advocating for open borders, free muleya of

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:55.230
labor.

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:56.240
I just don't see that.

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:00.949
Well, what has happened what has happened
is that the left in America and elsewhere

00:06:00.949 --> 00:06:06.370
has been finding it increasingly hard to react
against a specific.

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:07.790
I think I agree with you.

00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:09.919
Rather unusual narrative on the left wing.

00:06:09.919 --> 00:06:13.150
Nevertheless, the left is finding it increasingly
hard to resist.

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:16.880
I mean, obvious things.

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:22.620
People who come to the US illegally, amnesties,
these sorts of things.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:23.930
There has become a tendency.

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:29.130
I think it's it's a shame for the immigration
debate to be seen in the Left-Right demands.

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:33.510
It didn't used to be, of course, the people
on the left like Bernie Sanders, who used

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:37.720
to recognize the labor implications, more
of this sort of thing.

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:40.650
But we just don't get stuck on borders, the
time being.

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:46.910
My point is simply that the president has
a stricter policy than his opponents on immigration,

00:06:46.910 --> 00:06:48.730
rather boasts about it, if anything.

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:56.810
He clearly advocates the law and order agenda
defending police rather than any truck with

00:06:56.810 --> 00:06:59.070
people who say they want to defund them.

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:05.360
Look, I think, as I say, it's Donald Trump
is a very difficult person to draw any clear

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:09.810
lessons from, because obviously these things
that I've just mentioned, many others are

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:11.050
central to his platform.

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:15.490
They've been central to a lot of conservative
voters voting for him.

00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:19.410
And yet he is a totally unusual beast in nature.

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:26.530
And I suppose that, you know, one of the sadnesses
to me of the last four years is been the unwillingness

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:31.220
to learn the lessons of what the electorate
might have been saying for years ago in voting

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:36.630
for somebody who so many people had so many
problems with, not least on his own side,

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:41.380
that if you voted in the United States, would
you be voting for Donald Trump's reelection?

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:46.370
One of the great pleasures of not being a
citizen of the United States is not having

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:50.139
to weigh in in any way on the fractional politics.

00:07:50.139 --> 00:07:53.340
And so, as an outsider, I will dodge that
question.

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:55.970
So it's just not a question you answer.

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:57.760
It's not a question I answer.

00:07:57.760 --> 00:07:59.060
I'm not an American.

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:03.330
And I don't presume to say how I would vote
if I was an American.

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:07.290
I wouldn't like to advise any Americans on
how they should vote.

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:12.090
I can see the eye, can see a lot of arguments
each way.

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:16.230
But I think unless you live in a country and
pay taxes in a country and experience the

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:20.091
life in that country, I think you have to
be very wary and saying how people in that

00:08:20.091 --> 00:08:21.520
country should vote.

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:25.320
That's a lesson I think I would I'd love to
think talk more about that, because like,

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:28.770
for example, I was born in Argentina, but
I don't live there.

00:08:28.770 --> 00:08:33.399
But I'm very comfortable saying if I did,
I would not have voted for the current president

00:08:33.399 --> 00:08:38.810
or, you know, in Israel, I would not be a
supporter of Benjamin Netanyahu or in the

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:39.810
UK.

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:41.169
I would not be a supporter of Boris Johnson.

00:08:41.169 --> 00:08:45.589
And, you know, I'm not suggesting to anybody
else that I know more than them or telling

00:08:45.589 --> 00:08:46.589
them how to vote.

00:08:46.589 --> 00:08:49.910
But what why is it that you find it inappropriate
to comment on something like that?

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:52.230
We're a few days out from the election.

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:56.009
I write on these issues, but I don't like
to opine on how people should vote.

00:08:56.009 --> 00:08:57.670
I just don't do it.

00:08:57.670 --> 00:08:58.670
That's interesting.

00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:00.301
You know, it's it's just the principle on
it.

00:09:00.301 --> 00:09:01.301
I do.

00:09:01.301 --> 00:09:05.360
I primarily think that out, you know, outsiders
in a country shouldn't do that.

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:09.129
And what is the principle that leads you to
that conclusion?

00:09:09.129 --> 00:09:10.779
Well, I think I just said it.

00:09:10.779 --> 00:09:15.300
I don't think that if you don't live in the
country, don't pay taxes and much more.

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:20.129
But you really should on advising people in
any way how to vote, which obviously saying

00:09:20.129 --> 00:09:22.740
how you would vote in a country that does
that.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:27.939
But I think I mean, the one thing I would
say, obviously, is it's a it's a it's a very

00:09:27.939 --> 00:09:31.680
tricky situation that your country, the United
States, is in now.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:38.269
And I think that it's a very serious time,
not just to do with Donald Trump, I think

00:09:38.269 --> 00:09:43.059
is a very serious time in American history
and a difficult corner that you're at.

00:09:43.059 --> 00:09:53.559
To the extent that there is a sort of wing
of the electorate and this exists significantly

00:09:53.559 --> 00:10:01.319
in online circles that cross country lines,
you see a sort of coalescing around certain

00:10:01.319 --> 00:10:07.489
ideas about identity politics and free speech
suppression.

00:10:07.489 --> 00:10:12.149
Sometimes it includes discussions of tech
monopolies or social media networks.

00:10:12.149 --> 00:10:16.389
Sometimes the idea of a culture war is it's
sort of raised.

00:10:16.389 --> 00:10:21.610
I think you sort of understand that the sort
of umbrella that I'm referring to.

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:27.829
How important do you think that is in relation
to what we sometimes consider the sort of

00:10:27.829 --> 00:10:32.379
major policy debates around things like health
care?

00:10:32.379 --> 00:10:35.070
You mentioned immigration, foreign policy.

00:10:35.070 --> 00:10:36.240
Are these.

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.139
Do they overlap significantly?

00:10:38.139 --> 00:10:42.759
Are these groups of people that are concerned
with very, very different things and aren't

00:10:42.759 --> 00:10:45.149
as worried about those big policy questions?

00:10:45.149 --> 00:10:47.410
What's the interaction there?

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:48.410
All of these things shift.

00:10:48.410 --> 00:10:49.410
Don't they?

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:50.470
All the time.

00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:52.819
Foreign policy in particular, which you mentioned,
is a good example.

00:10:52.819 --> 00:10:57.809
There are there are parties that have been
very interventionist in the United States

00:10:57.809 --> 00:11:02.439
and in the United Kingdom in my lifetime,
which within years become relatively isolationist.

00:11:02.439 --> 00:11:05.569
You know, all of these things shift around.

00:11:05.569 --> 00:11:09.649
No political party remains totally static,
albeit they like to give out the impression

00:11:09.649 --> 00:11:11.739
very often they do.

00:11:11.739 --> 00:11:14.420
And I think that it's very hard to draw these
trends across borders.

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:15.420
I think you're right.

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:18.660
I think one thing that is happening in all
of our countries clearly is that there is

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:25.250
this uncomfortable disconnect going on between
the let's see, the traditional political wars

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:28.110
which have always gone on and which always
will go on.

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:34.129
And then these these larger, you might even
say to some extent, deeper US sort of identity

00:11:34.129 --> 00:11:37.639
issues that are going on underneath everybody.

00:11:37.639 --> 00:11:43.309
These ones are and some people call these
culture wars and much more.

00:11:43.309 --> 00:11:49.999
These are very tricky things for politics
to address because you can do them sort of

00:11:49.999 --> 00:11:50.999
glibly.

00:11:50.999 --> 00:11:54.339
You can sort of nod to them.

00:11:54.339 --> 00:11:58.670
But most politicians find them very dangerous
to touch.

00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:02.779
That's certainly the experience in my own
country, the British politics, is that there

00:12:02.779 --> 00:12:07.610
are these deep issues which occasionally I
think of them like a sort of geezer.

00:12:07.610 --> 00:12:12.279
They don't mean it in the Cockney Guy Ritchie
sentence.

00:12:12.279 --> 00:12:19.160
I mean, there's a type of geezer thing that
happens where a moment allows the jet to come

00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:24.749
up and suddenly everybody notices it and you
notice what's going on underneath.

00:12:24.749 --> 00:12:28.619
One of the interesting things that happens
with the interaction of politics is what you

00:12:28.619 --> 00:12:34.069
describe with this sort of online world is
that these things occasionally erupt and people

00:12:34.069 --> 00:12:35.069
can sort of refer to them.

00:12:35.069 --> 00:12:37.139
They don't know what to do with them otherwise.

00:12:37.139 --> 00:12:39.470
I mean, you can have an issue.

00:12:39.470 --> 00:12:40.470
Oh, I don't know.

00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:41.970
There'll be a specific people.

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:47.470
I mean, I think a council culture is an interesting
one to think about where you've had a problem

00:12:47.470 --> 00:12:51.480
with online now for a couple of years, there
have been, you know, all of these debates

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:57.029
happening about what does one have a right
to speak at a college or or not?

00:12:57.029 --> 00:12:58.589
And what does it mean if there are protests?

00:12:58.589 --> 00:13:00.350
And what about their speech?

00:13:00.350 --> 00:13:05.709
And then it led over to where Donald Trump
has sent some of his wildly capitalized tweets,

00:13:05.709 --> 00:13:07.679
where he talks about council culture.

00:13:07.679 --> 00:13:11.410
It's sort of pushed slightly above the surface
into something.

00:13:11.410 --> 00:13:12.889
Trump is talking about.

00:13:12.889 --> 00:13:14.389
Is that maybe an example?

00:13:14.389 --> 00:13:15.389
Yes, it is.

00:13:15.389 --> 00:13:16.389
It is.

00:13:16.389 --> 00:13:18.110
It's not a bad example.

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:23.999
I would say, again, it's it's a good example
in some ways because you have that row about

00:13:23.999 --> 00:13:25.199
that specific thing.

00:13:25.199 --> 00:13:27.999
For instance, should X person speak Y venue?

00:13:27.999 --> 00:13:29.410
Yes, but you do.

00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:32.670
But we struggle with this question of France's.

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:39.239
Why are so many people in our time finding
it so incredibly hard to hear a contrary opinion?

00:13:39.239 --> 00:13:42.040
What would the limits of opinion be?

00:13:42.040 --> 00:13:46.980
Can they be in the public sphere, the same
thing that they are online?

00:13:46.980 --> 00:13:50.299
These are all questions that we've been forced
to.

00:13:50.299 --> 00:13:55.759
More or less think about and very imperfectly
address because of the strange online world

00:13:55.759 --> 00:14:05.649
which we all live with.

00:14:05.649 --> 00:15:00.600
We're going 

00:15:00.600 --> 00:16:34.870
to pause my conversation with Douglas Murray
there.

00:16:34.870 --> 00:17:18.130
The full interview will be available on our
YouTube channel very soon at YouTube dot com

00:17:18.130 --> 00:17:30.780
slash The David Pakman Show.

00:17:30.780 --> 00:18:05.210
We'll take 

00:18:05.210 --> 00:18:37.300
a quick break and 

00:18:37.300 --> 00:28:42.910
be back after this.

